that's then who gave us the ability&nbsp;
to to get the film made because her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.280
name has value and then it becomes&nbsp;
an actual kind of a real thing.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.520
Film Courage: What is a&nbsp;
proof of concept short film?

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.120
Jesse: A proof of concept short film is basically&nbsp;
making a short or it could be something even like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.680
a teaser or trailer but basically showing what&nbsp;
you can do and what you want to do in the feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:37.440
version. In my case, I had written this feature&nbsp;
script that I wanted to make, but I hadn't made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.040
I made this first feature when I was 17. I was&nbsp;
now I don't know 34 at the time when I was trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.360
to make this feature and I didn't really have any&nbsp;
kind of recent narrative work to show. I had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.680
making commercials for the last almost 10 years&nbsp;
but a lot of that isn't really transferable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.520
narrative storytelling in the same way and so I&nbsp;
knew I needed to basically prove not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.760
to myself but to the people that were going&nbsp;
to fund this film that I could make this film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.040
A lot of people were telling me that too and so&nbsp;
I decided to basically make a short film kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.920
inspired from my feature that would show the style&nbsp;
and the tone and the vibe I was going for and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:27.280
showcase my filmmaking as a director. I think that&nbsp;
is super important for young filmmakers because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.320
unless you have a whole collection of really great&nbsp;
short films and even if you do it's the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.400
style or tone than the feature a lot of times&nbsp;
people just won't have the faith that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.200
do that and that's a separate issue in general.&nbsp;
Hollywood probably should have a lot more faith in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.280
people and take some more risks but unfortunately&nbsp;
they don't and so you really kind of have to prove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.720
to people that you can do this. For me to get my&nbsp;
feature off the ground it was essential to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:59.280
made this proof of concept because it was probably&nbsp;
a 12-15 minute long film. I had taken kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.360
a character from the feature and wrote a kind&nbsp;
of back story for them and it was so it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.560
a scene from the film it wasn't a teaser but it&nbsp;
was kind of like a almost a prequel to the feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.400
of kind of an origin story of onebof the&nbsp;
characters in the feature and so it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.080
be its own standalone thing the nice thing about&nbsp;
doing that way is it could be its own short it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.560
could live on its own even if the feature never&nbsp;
happened or did happen it could still exist it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.880
wasn't like a teaser or a trailer or something&nbsp;
like that it was a full film short film but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.960
totally in alignment to the feature in terms of&nbsp;
the cinematography the pacing the kind of acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.120
just the whole style I was trying to create&nbsp;
and showed that I could pull off these scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.880
and it had a little bit of action but but it kind&nbsp;
of showed that kind of my ability as a director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.680
and it just opened up so many more doors in terms&nbsp;
of people don't want to read scripts everyone has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.640
a script even if you're just good they still&nbsp;
don't want to read it probably because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.880
don't think it's good and so but if you can show&nbsp;
them if you can send them a link and show them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.920
a 12 or 15 minute short film that's compelling&nbsp;
and has some talent to it then they'll be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.080
okay that's actually pretty good actually I will&nbsp;
read this script now and and then that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.440
kind of started to for me build more and more&nbsp;
opportunities for getting my feature made was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.040
was then people would watch the short and then&nbsp;
they would read the script and I also had like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.360
a deck that I went with along all this where it&nbsp;
was like it was my kind of vision and take for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.000
how to make the movie in terms of breaking down&nbsp;
the cinematography the music the editing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.840
kind of skills I learned from making commercials&nbsp;
where you have to kind of make a a treatment or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.840
pitch for why you should direct that commercial&nbsp;
it was that same kind of thing but for my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.160
movie of like what it would look and feel like&nbsp;
so together that was kind of my package I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.880
send to people and I didn't have any agent or&nbsp;
manager or barely knew anyone I was just kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.440
taking any kind of connections and relationships&nbsp;
I did have and seeing where it could go from there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.280
and it took a long time it took several years and&nbsp;
eventually someone would get it and they'd pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:06.320
on to somebody else and in my case a producer&nbsp;
saw the short really liked it then he read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.120
script he really liked that and he brought in his&nbsp;
casting director that he works with all the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.200
and together they were able to get the&nbsp;
script out to some of the the bigger agencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.400
and that's eventually how we got to Lucy Hale&nbsp;
who stars in the film and that's a then who gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.880
us the ability to get the film made because her&nbsp;
name has value and then it becomes an actual kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.240
of a real thing and so none of that would have&nbsp;
happened without this proof of concept short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.520
so was the backstory about was it Elly is that her&nbsp;
character no it was one of the other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

